---
args: { input: CaseInput }
returns: RunProposal
---
1. Bind `goal = await frameRequest(input)` from the request and `policy = await selectPolicy(input)` from supplied authority candidates. Preserve unresolved precedence instead of guessing which source governs.
2. Bind `work = await formTaskGraph(goal)` and `evidencePlan = await planEvidence(input, work)` so each operation has explicit prerequisites and a searched source universe.
3. Run the stages in their dependency order. The first stage receives only `input.domainRecords.firstStage`; every later stage receives the preceding function's actual accepted records. Bind each full result separately.
const interpretChangesResult = await interpretChanges(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan);
const mapConsumersResult = await mapConsumers(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, interpretChangesResult.accepted);
const replayCompatibilityResult = await replayCompatibility(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, mapConsumersResult.accepted);
const planMigrationResult = await planMigration(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, replayCompatibilityResult.accepted);
const coordinateReleaseResult = await coordinateRelease(input, goal, policy, evidencePlan, planMigrationResult.accepted);
4. Bind `stageSet: CaseStageSet = { interpretChanges: interpretChangesResult, mapConsumers: mapConsumersResult, replayCompatibility: replayCompatibilityResult, planMigration: planMigrationResult, coordinateRelease: coordinateReleaseResult }` and `collection: CollectionFinding = await reconcileCollection(stageSet)`. Bind `allQuestions: string[] = [...interpretChangesResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...mapConsumersResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...replayCompatibilityResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...planMigrationResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...coordinateReleaseResult.nextQuestions.map(question => question.question), ...collection.coverageGaps]` and `questions: QuestionBatch = await resolveQuestions(allQuestions, input)`.
5. Bind `invalidation = await assessDelta(input)`, `review = await prepareReview(collection)`, and `view = await composeView(review, input)`; pass each child its actual declared inputs and retain the returned object.
6. Bind `allChecks: CheckResult[] = [...interpretChangesResult.checks, ...mapConsumersResult.checks, ...replayCompatibilityResult.checks, ...planMigrationResult.checks, ...coordinateReleaseResult.checks]`. Bind `hasGateFailure = allChecks.some(check => check.verdict !== "pass")` and `hasBlocker = input.authorityStatus !== "authorized" || questions.questions.length > 0 || collection.coverageGaps.length > 0`. Bind `disposition: "proposed" | "blocked" | "needs-review" = hasBlocker ? "blocked" : hasGateFailure ? "needs-review" : "proposed"`. Bind `rationale: string[] = allChecks.map(check => `${check.check}: ${check.verdict}; evidence ${check.evidenceIds.join(",")}`)`.
7. Operational detail 1 — A default changes without a schema change: include default-dependent consumer scenarios. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (include default-dependent consumer scenarios.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralChange; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralChange, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
8. Operational detail 2 — An integration is unowned: block risky migration and escalate ownership. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (block risky migration and escalate ownership.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralChange; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralChange, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
9. Operational detail 3 — A customer pins an old version: preserve its supported behavior and migration window. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (preserve its supported behavior and migration window.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralChange; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralChange, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
10. Operational detail 4 — Rollback would lose newly written data: require an explicit data-compatibility plan. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (require an explicit data-compatibility plan.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralChange; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralChange, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
11. Operational detail 5 — Documentation publishes early: label the applicable release and prevent misleading current guidance. When the current evidence establishes this detail, apply its stated domain action (label the applicable release and prevent misleading current guidance.) through the existing stage result for BehavioralChange; preserve exact source/version and the prior BehavioralChange, expose the affected item and uncertainty in the existing review/view output, represent changes as typed versioned proposals, and keep consequential operations pending the existing human approval boundary. Never mutate a system of record or claim an external effect here.
12. Initialize `revisions: RevisionProposal[] = []`. If any gate is not pass, bind `revision = await proposeRevision(the failed check names, the smallest affected stage name)` and add that returned proposal to revisions. Bind and return `result: RunProposal = { caseId: input.caseId, goal, policy, work, evidencePlan, interpretChanges: interpretChangesResult, mapConsumers: mapConsumersResult, replayCompatibility: replayCompatibilityResult, planMigration: planMigrationResult, coordinateRelease: coordinateReleaseResult, collection, questions, invalidation, review, view, revisions, disposition, rationale }`. Proposed actions remain proposals. The host owns authorization, persistence, effect dispatch and verification.
